observes that if we understand race as “a conglomerate of sociopolitical relations that discipline humanity into full humans, not- quite- humans, and nonhumans, then blackness designates a changing system of unequal power structures that apportion and delimit which humans can lay claim to full human status and which humans cannot” (3). Weheliye then expands on the work of Black feminists Hortense Spillers and Sylvia Wynter to argue that Black studies works to decolonize the category of human through its exploration of the nonhuman to critique what has been centered as physically, racially, and sexually normative – “human.” Morrison’s use of nonhuman images and figures plays on this criticism while employing the nonhuman as a sign of the physical and sexual violence visited upon Black bodies throughout the entirety of U.S. history as well as the social death associated with Black non/being. Further, it operates as rhetorical tactic that allows Morrison to engage with this same critique of the “human” through evoking what bell hooks calls the oppositional gaze, with the lens not resting voyeuristically on the gratuitous violence against Black bodies but on the inhumanity of a Whiteness that actively participates in and perpetuates a system where Paul D looks up to Mister.1 Cornelius Eady mirrors this aspect of Morrison’s writing in his 2001 poetry collection, Brutal Imagination. Much like Morrison’s employment of the nonhuman, Eady’s conjuring of a nonhuman persona facilitates an oppositional gaze focused on White supremacy and how its violences build from the ontological to the structural. Placing Eady within Morrison’s wake works in several respects. Eady is often noted, and rightly so, for his co-founding of Cave Canem with Toi Derricotte. Founded in 1996, Cave Canem has grown from a 26-poet workshop organized “to remedy the under-representation and isolation” of Black poets in the U.S. literary landscape to one of the most influential and potent institutions in U.S. American poetics, with its fellows going on to win many of the most prestigious awards in the poetry world (“Mission and History”). As such, it would be accurate to liken Eady to Morrison through their shared focus on increasing the volume – both audibility and quantity – of Black literary voices. That said, Eady accumulated the social capital needed to help establish Cave Canem and help build it into one of the most prominent forces in poetry through putting in poetic work. Before finishing his MFA at Warren Wilson, Eady had published Kartunes (1980), earned an NEA, and became the second Black poet to win a Lamont Poetry Prize (now the James Laughlin Award) for his second collection, Victims of the Latest Dance Craze (1986). His third collection, The Gathering of My Name (1991), was nominated for a Pulitzer and was followed by You Don’t Miss Your Water (1995), the autobiography of
