against her own children, which echoes the Gardner narrative, but the circumstances – the physical, psychological, sexual, and other violences that were heaped upon the enslaved, including having one’s “human characteristics” listed along with one’s “animal ones” (Morrison, Beloved 228) – that brought Sethe to make such an awful decision in order to save her children from slavery. Similar to Sethe’s story, what brings the Smith story into the unspeakable is not Smith’s murder of her children – an act that was no doubt much less selfless than Sethe’s – but her evocation of and participation in the systems that construct Eady’s Why, the same ones that, through the ontological violence of the myth of Black criminality, feed and justify racial hoaxers like Smith and the state violence and racial terrorism that the hoaxers sic on Black(ened) peoples. As such, the cycle’s primary speaker embodies Eady’s quandary as the personification of this corner of what Morrison referred to as the “Africanist presence” as a means to underline and critique how Blackness is constructed in the White literary imagination, and in the broader racial imaginary, as “a fabricated brew of darkness, otherness, alarm, and desire” (Morrison, “Black Matter(s)” 147). The “Brutal Imagination” cycle opens with “How I Got Born” and its epigraph, which introduces the cycle’s primary speaker, later named Mr. Zero, as “the young black man Susan Smith claimed kidnapped her children” (5). Eady immediately complicates that seemingly simple sentence, revealing Mr. Zero’s existence did not begin with Smith and proving the poem’s title to be an intentional misnomer, a bit of ironic trickery:
Though it’s common belief That Susan Smith willed me alive At the moment Her babies sank into the lake
When called, I come. My job is to get things done (5)
Mr. Zero is not born in Susan Smith’s car, or even in her mind. He is merely “called,” clocking in, and Smith just happens to be the one employing him in this instance. In a personal interview with Eady, he deconstructs Susan Smith’s decision to claim a Black man stole her car and abducted her sons:
In the Susan Smith story, there are two constructs. One is the invention of the African American villain, but she is also constructing a victim that looks like the imaginary guy. She wants the authorities to look for somebody who is the closest to her nightmare . . . The second construct got thwarted, thank God, but that’s what she was doing. She was inventing a plausible villain, but she was also inventing a plausible victim, so that somebody gets on death row, because he fits the profile that she is constructing . . . We are lucky that it didn’t work out that way, but that is what she was going for, and it has worked that way. (Personal Interview)
