In addition to drawing the correlation between the plausible villain and plausible victim, Eady connects Mr. Zero to recent iterations of the Black brute trope, or what Katheryn Russell-Brown refers to as “the myth of the criminalblackman” (3) in her The Color of Crime: Racial Hoaxes, White Fear, Black Protectionism, Police Harassment, and Other Macroaggressions (1998). Like the Black brute, Russell-Brown’s criminalblackman was formed from and broadcasted by the media’s disproportionate and hyperbolized portrayals of Black deviance. This “entrapment by media imagery” has effectively fixed the association of Black men with crime in the racial imaginary and, furthermore, is evidence of representations of Black people as pathologically criminal which has operated as a means to execute and justify antiBlack agendas (Russell-Brown xiii). Further, Eady’s personification of Mr. Zero more specifically links the nonhuman speaker to the long line of White women falsely accusing Black men and boys that runs from no doubt many unnamed enslaved and free Black men who were lynched because they fit the description of the plausible villain/victim, and includes Emmett Till and his accuser, Carolyn Bryant Donham; and the more recent Amanda Knox case and her lying on Patrick Lumumba, and so on seemingly ad infinitum.3 Yet, Eady also ties Smith to Charles Stuart – a White man who shot and killed his pregnant wife and blamed it on a Black man in 1989 – in “Charles Stuart in the Hospital” (22). Thus, Eady’s use of the nonhuman immediately engages the reader in the past and present of the myth criminalblackman as a racist idea that further propagates the ontological violence that Eady describes in the constructs of the plausible villain and plausible victim.4
The palatable Blackness of the plausible villain
After the first six lines problematize the notion that Smith birthed Mr. Zero, the next six lines of “How I Got Born” discuss Smith’s particular evocation of Mr. Zero and individual need for the plausible villain. The lines read:
I am piecemeal. I make my living by taking things.
