So now a mother needs me clothed In hand-me-downs And a knit cap. (5)
Here Mr. Zero describes Smith quilting him out of tropes she pulled from the racial imaginary. Smith’s two requests for his uniform are signs of the pathological Blackness – “hand-me-downs” signaling a lower socioeconomic status and a “knit cap” pointing toward criminality. In her confession, Smith revealed that she planned to commit a murder-suicide but jumped out of the car at the last minute and ran with her hands over her ears so “she would not hear her sons cries or the car splashing into the lake” (Rekers 86). The Washington Post quoted an unidentified police source who recounted to The Post in 1994 that “Every part of [Smith’s] world was falling apart, and one thing led to another . . . There doesn’t seem to have been great plans aforethought in her actions. They just happened” (Iati). Yet, while the source’s comments seem to imply the racialized description of her imagined carjacker to have “just happened,” the history of racial hoaxes tells us differently. Instead, Smith’s “piecemeal” (5) description in “How I Got Born” reflects the education described in the poem “My Face”:
. . .when you were Of a certain age Someone pointed A finger In the wrong direction
And said: All they do Is fuck and drink All they’re good for Ain’t worth a shit. (10-11)
This is further evidenced when one considers how the sketch artist to whom Smith described her fictional perpetrator initially reported him wearing a baseball cap, then Smith switched it to a knit cap, seemingly as an attempt to further cement the figure’s imagined Blackness.5 Instead, Smith’s imagined Black carjacker and kidnapper reveals Smith as literate enough in stereotypical imagery of Black people that she was able pull these tropes from out of thin air despite the immediate trauma of having just murdered her own children.
