A key rhetorical strategy of the dominant culture is the framing of racial hoaxers like Smith as wayward individuals not representative of the greater public. We see this strategy in other racialized phenomena as well – police officers and White supremacists who brutalize and kill BIPOCs, judges and prosecutors who have been found to have unjustly prosecuted and/or sentenced BIPOCs, politicians who push policy with racist outcomes who are outed for making their bigotry too visible or public, educators and educational administrators who champion racist curriculums and/or pedagogies who are caught being too openly racist in the classroom or on social media, and all the other ways in which the public finds it more comfortable to understand individuals as “bad apples” as opposed to seeing them as active agents of traditions supporting and supported by systemic racism. This is often done by othering the individuals and their actions through the use of ableist language like “crazy” and “insane.” Smith does this herself in her confession, and her mental state was an integral part of her defense.6 Twenty years after the murders, The State reporter Harrison Cahill reached out to Smith to get her take on how time has impacted her feelings about what she did. In her response, she explains to Cahill that “I was not in my right mind.” She writes that “I am not the monster that society thinks I am,” and “The only reason I lied is because I didn’t know how to tell the people who loved Michael and Alex that they would never see them again” (qtd. in Cahill). Her letter makes it clear that Smith, even twenty years later, is concerned with describing her mental and emotional state at the time of the murders; however, she offers little in the way of explaining why she told the particular lie she told. Ultimately, her lie demonstrates her clear understanding that, even in the moments immediately following her own murderous act, the fictional Black monster – the plausible villain – would give her story the highest degree of believability (and, in at least her opinion, would be the most palatable story for those who loved her sons). Further, her claims about her state of mind, however valid or invalid, operate as an attempt to distance herself from the culpability of the murders as well as from her racial hoaxing. Therí A. Pickens resists this tactic in her essay “Amy Cooper is Logical.” In the essay, Pickens responds to a more recent racial hoax in which Amy Cooper a White woman – attempted to weaponize her White license and the police against Christian Cooper – a Black birdwatcher of no relation – after he had
