asked her to follow park rules and leash her dog. Pickens resists responses to the episode painting Amy Cooper as “crazy” as “not just a rhetorical move; it is part of a cultural end-game that creates safety for those who want to put rhetorical and psychological distance between themselves and Amy Cooper.” “Crazy” functions as a tactic reducing the racial hoax to an act of interpersonal racism, while denying the systemic racism that is both the backbone and blood of the hoax. Framing the racial hoaxers, the Amy Coopers and Susan Smiths, “as individual actors, not part the American fabric” (Pickens) goes beyond color-evasive racism in its denial of the implicit and explicit bias that makes Black men low hanging fruit for the racial hoaxer and does not adequately implicate those who are all too eager to embrace the racial hoax. As George Rekers chronicles in Susan Smith: Victim or Murderer (1996), folks from Smith’s husband, David Smith, to her lover, Tom Findlay, to folks working in or with the police department and media questioned Smith’s affect, prioritization of Findlay, the details of and inconsistencies in her story from day one, yet that did not stop the incredibly expensive and extensive nine-day search.7 Which is to say, when Susan Smith described a Black man to the police sketch artist, she was operating from, and speaking into, a clear logic. And that logic, despite the questionability of Smith’s story, was readily embraced and imbibed on a national level. Eady’s use of the rhetorical and metaphoric potential of the nonhuman persona is particularly evident and effective in his pronoun play in the “Brutal Imagination” cycle. This is first signaled by the formal break that takes place between the penultimate and final stanzas of “How I Got Born.” The first two stanzas are neat quatrains that run pretty congruently. They both feature a second line that rises out of a shorter first line before collapsing into a four-syllable third line from which the fourth line expands to match or exceed the length of the first two lines. Both stanzas also feature a Mr. Zero who uses first-person pronouns for himself and third-person for Smith that clearly distinguish Mr. Zero from Smith: “Though it’s common belief/That Susan Smith willed me alive/At the moment/Her babies sank into the lake/When called, I come./My job is to get things done” (5; use of bold typeface is mine). The penultimate stanza is consistent in its point of view, yet the final line of its quatrain has been absorbed into the hinge that occurs at the beginning of the final stanza – “Whatever” (5) – inflating the final stanza to five lines, while also announcing the formal break that introduces the conflation of Smith and Mr. Zero into a collective “we”: “We arrive, bereaved/On the stranger’s step” (5). A superficial reading of this line might imagine Mr. Zero and Smith as separate entities grouped
