together through the “we,” but, as we know, Smith did not arrive on that “stranger’s step” with a Black man in tow, or even an actual Black man in mind. This pronoun play continues in the we’s and our’s that are prominent in the first and third sections of the cycle as well as the final two lines of the poem that makes up the fourth and final section, “Birthing.” Reading “Who I Am?” through this lens helps to address and further complicate the poem’s titular question and, moreover, to further illuminate the efficacy of Eady’s use of pronouns and persona. In the poem, Eady imagines the scene in Smith’s burgundy 1990 Mazda Protégé before she drowns Michael and Alex Smith. Mr. Zero is driving, and the boys are buckled into their seats, with the opening line attributed to one of boys who wonders: “Who are you, mister?” (7; italics present in original). The rest of the poem comes in the way of a response. Similar to “How I Got Born,” the first twothirds of the poem presents a clear distinction between Smith and Mr. Zero through Mr. Zero’s use of first- and third-person pronouns that begin in the poem’s fourth line: “If I am alive, then so, briefly, are they” (7). The “three and one” of the next line is a literal reference to Michael’s and Alex’s ages, but it also doubles as a sort of inventory of who is in the car – the three Smiths and Mr. Zero – all of them “Quiet and scared, bunched together/Breathing like small beasts” (7). Though the pronouns still indicate the bifurcation of Smith and Mr. Zero, the two start to merge as the poem progresses. Where before there was confusion about Mr. Zero’s identity, now there is recognition:
They can’t place me, yet there’s Something familiar. Though my skin and sex are different, maybe It’s the way I drive Or occasionally glance back With concern, (7)
Mr. Zero is still different from Susan Smith here, but Mr. Zero is beyond “familiar;” particularly as he exhibits (presumably) Smith’s maternal mannerisms. Eady is imagining the boys seeing their mother in Mr. Zero and Mr. Zero in their mother. The “mixed” in the following line carries weight in the meaning of the poem as it indicates the conflation of Smith and Mr. Zero. The pronouns mirror this through the switch from the “I/me” and “they” from before to the “us” and “we”:
Maybe it’s the mixed blessing Someone, perhaps circumstance, Has given us, The secret thrill hiding, Childish, in plain sight,
