Seen, but not seen, As if suddenly given the power To move through walls, To know every secret without permission. We roll sleepless through the dark streets, but inside The cab is lit with brutal imagination. (7)
The pronoun play constructs a rich dualism. The persona “I” allows Mr. Zero to embody the immediate persona subject – in this case Smith’s specific evocation of the criminalblackman – while also serving as a shorthand for a larger set of ideas. In this sense, Eady’s use of persona in the “Brutal Imagination” cycle creates a space where Mr. Zero’s “us” refers to both the trope as an individual occurrence as well as how the trope operates historically and contemporaneously in a society all too familiar with the Black-manas-plausible-villain narrative. The persona “I” also enables the dualism between Smith and Mr. Zero. The conflation of Smith and Mr. Zero further demonstrates Mr. Zero as the personification of the Black trope of the criminalblackman on one level; however, on another deeper level he is also a part of Smith as a figment of the “brutal [White] imagination.” The dualism fostered through the use of persona troubles the dominant culture’s desire to write off Smith as operating out of her own individual dysfunction and instead exposes Smith as metonymic of a system of White supremacy that has constructed in its imagination the Black monster, the plausible villain, as well as what all that has been done in the name of Whiteness to cement such a narrative in such a way that it can be so easily, and legibly, deployed. Crucial to Morrison’s and Eady’s intersecting use of the nonhuman is how they employ the nonhuman as a tactic to construct an oppositional gaze that reads Whiteness into hypervisibility. Miguel Rivera’s essay “The Decentering of White Experience in Cornelius Eady’s Brutal Imagination” offers an interesting reading of the “Brutal Imagination” cycle, arguing that Smith and Mr. Zero are joined through their bereavement, linked together “Just as Peter Pan staples his shadow to his feet.” Rivera uses this imagery to argue that “Eady recontextualizes the tragedy and decenters Smith;” furthermore, the “sequence decenters whiteness and the white sensory experience.” Instead, Mr. Zero “is the center of the narrative and the source of all existential insight offered by the sequence” as he personifies the “a previously dismal category of the white-imagined disposition of the Black American.” Some of this jives with the reading presented in this article, but the idea of Whiteness being decentered does not. Rivera anchors this argument in a famous James Baldwin quote from the 1964 documentary Take this Hammer where Baldwin gets at the heart of the racial imaginary: “What I think of you as being is dictated by my own necessity, my own psychology, my own fears and desires . . . Now here in this country we’ve got something called the ‘nigger’ . . . I didn’t invent him, White people invented him . . . I’ve always
