known that I’m not a ‘nigger.’ But if I am not the ‘nigger’ and if it’s true that your invention reveals you, then who is the ‘nigger’?” Rivera further evidences this stance with his observation that the majority of the cycle’s poems that conflate Smith and Mr. Zero imagine them housed within Mr. Zero’s fictionalized Black body with the only significant deviations coming in the cycle’s third section following “Press Conference” – where the reader watches as “Susan steps to the mike,” but hears a “a crack in our voice” (Eady 46). In “Confession,” which immediately precedes the fourth section poem “Birthing,” Mr. Zero reads like a demon possessing Smith that “has almost/Spilled/From her,” a phantom that “ . . . well[s]/Under/Her eyelids” (48). These poems, instead, highlight Smith as Mr. Zero’s host, struggling to keep Mr. Zero hidden, suppressing him within her as he slides “Between her teeth,/Double/Her over as she tries/Not/To blurt me out” (49). However, we might better read the conflation of Mr. Zero and Smith through Sharpe’s discussion of Black annotation and Black redaction “as trans*verse and coextensive ways to imagine otherwise” in the midst of the historical and ongoing ontological violence committed through the imaging and imagining of Blackness and Black bodies (115). Sharpe further describes this as working
toward reading and seeing something in the excess of what is caught in the frame; towards seeing something beyond a visuality that is, as Nicholas Mirzoeff (2011) argues, subtended by the logics of the administered plantation. In “Home,” Toni Morrison (1998, 7) writes that she has consistently tried ‘to carve away the accretions of the seat, blindness, ignorance, paralysis, and share malevolence embedded in racist language so the other kinds of perception we’re not only available but we’re inevitable.’ I am imagining that the work of Black annotation and Black redaction is to enact the movement to that inevitable—a counter to abandonment, another effort to try to look, to try to really see. (117)
Mr. Zero’s presence in and position as the primary speaker of the poems is in itself an act of Black annotation, increasing the visibility of Smith’s hoaxing. In this way, Mr. Zero’s annotation of the Smith case and the shift of the host body of the “I” from Mr. Zero to Smith’s body in the cycle’s latter poems is reminiscent of John Lucas’s erasure of Lawrence Beitler’s 1930 photograph of a public lynching featured in Claudia Rankine’s Citizen and Ken GonzalesDay’s “Erased Lynchings” series. Both projects take photographs of public lynchings – which were not just common, but part of a whole industry where people would pose for pictures at lynchings that were then turned into post cards that the pictured would send to their friends and loved ones – and erase the lynched bodies. These erasures highlight the White spectators, often with looks of pure glee, indifference, and other seemingly incongruent expressions, as well as, as Gonzales-Day points out, “the social conditions that made these racialized killings possible” (Anderson). Stamp Paid’s “the jungle” analogy in Beloved presents another Morrison analog. When reflecting on the haunting taking place in 124, Stamp Paid uses the nonhuman in the form of “the jungle”
