as a means to describe the racist construction of Blackness that drives White supremacy so that Stamp Paid cannot think of a Black person who “had lived a livable life” (234). While initially Stamp Paid describes “the jungle,” a reference to the dehumaning of Black peoples, as something “whitefolks planted in [Black people], the focus of the metaphor turns from the Black body and focuses on how “the screaming baboon lived under their own white skin” (234). Mr. Zero’s annotations have a similar rhetorical effect, redacting the Black body that Smith inserts into her hoax. At first glance, this function of Mr. Zero’s annotation seems to be antithetical to Sharpe’s theorization with her having defined Black annotation and redaction as “ways to make Black life visible” (123). However, what ultimately situates Eady’s use of Mr. Zero within the scope of Sharpe’s Black annotation and redaction, and thus also within her call to wake work, is how the intervention of Mr. Zero “disrupt[s] the dysgraphia that wrote a version of events that was riven with antiblackness” (123). Rivera is correct to point out that these poems signal how Mr. Zero operates as a sort of phantom that elucidates the racial imaginary; however, his assessment that this decenters Smith and Whiteness, while using Baldwin to support that analysis, seems to misunderstand Baldwin and the Why? at the heart of Eady’s persona project. Baldwin is not divesting from ‘nigger’ in order to decenter Whiteness. It is quite the opposite: he uses the invention to critique its inventor – Whiteness. This is a sentiment Toni Morrison later echoes while discussing the “profound neurosis” of those who subscribe to racist ideologies in a 1993 interview with Charlie Rose when she concludes that “My feeling is that white people have a very, very serious problem. And they should start thinking about what they can do about it. Take me out of it” (“Toni Morrison”; Morrison’s emphasis). Mirroring Morrison’s “Take me out of it” and Baldwin’s divestment, the shift of the “I” being housed in the fictionalized Black body to the real White body exhibits how Eady is, through Black annotation and redaction, centering Whiteness through an oppositional gaze that illuminates that it is Whiteness that found it necessary to invent Mr. Zero and that continues to maintain his job security. As a metaphorical representation of Eady’s Why?, and as a product of the “brutal imagination,” Mr. Zero may appear Black, but he is born of and from Whiteness. His work, as Eady re-deploys him, is not to push aside Whiteness, but to render it hypervisible.8
8GerShun Avilez presents a similar argument in the first chapter of his book Radical Aesthetics and Modern Black Nationalism (2016) where he states that: “What Eady actually presents in his work is his own imagining of this White figure imagining a black figure; he inhabits this White persona that is involved in the veritable creation of Black identity. The poetic cycle is as much about conceptions of Whiteness as it is about conceptions of Blackness” (54). He reads Smith’s conjuring of Mr. Zero as an “instance of catachrestic misnaming [that] illustrates how Whiteness performs itself as Blackness” (56). As such, Eady’s imagining of Smith summoning Mr. Zero effectively illustrates how Whiteness “makes itself invisible to ensure its presence. In enabling Susan’s innocence, the phantasmatic Zero represents the conceptual manifestation of both the rhetoric of [White] innocence and Black abstraction” (56).
WOMEN’S STUDIES 203


The palatable Blackness of the plausible victim
Many of the poems in Eady’s cycle center on Mr. Zero as plausible villain. The cycle then illuminates, through poems like “How I Got Born” and “Charles Stuart in the Hospital,” how Mr. Zero is signifyin’ on the legacy of racial hoaxers and their exploitation of Mr. Zero’s labor.9 Yet, what isn’t always on the surface of the poems is that every time Mr. Zero is called to work, there is a ripple effect sent through the Black communities in close proximity to his job site – both geographically and socially. Consequently, the plausible victim exists, and persists, in a causal relationship with the plausible villain, the victimization of Black peoples being a byproduct of racist ideas and actions that reify or capitalize on the belief that Black peoples are genetically and/or culturally less good, less citizen, less human.10 The intertwined histories of the racial hoax and the plausible Black victim runs at least as far back as the Redemption Era and its climate of racial terror, if not located in the origins of the Transatlantic slave trade. Instances of racialized violence as a result of a racial hoax were, and are, still very present particularly when we consider the racial hoax’s responsibility in bolstering the myth of the criminalblackman and how that, in turn, has played a direct role in the current weather of police violence and terror against Black folks. The Charles Stuart case referenced in “Charles Stuart in the Hospital” serves as a prime example with Stuart’s racial hoax leading to, and enabling, the Boston Police Department (BPD) to produce one of the first large-scale abuses of the stop-and-frisk policy, incarcerating two Black men and committing over 150 stop-and-frisks per day between October 24th and October 28th, 1989 with similar levels of searches occurring through November 1989 (Naughton). The emotional and psychological trauma of such a climate is difficult to quantify as it hardly ever stays put; it tends to haunt through its ripples, its echoes, its repetition.11 The state-sanctioned terror and humiliation brought on by, and that seeks justification in, the racial hoax speaks to another form of ontological violence. Instead of the ontological violence committed through the racist framing of the criminalblackman, this ontological violence proffers emotional
9Henry Louis Gates Jr. theorizes signifyin’ in The Signifying Monkey: A Theory of African-American Literary Criticism as, “Whatever is black about black American literature is to be found in this identifiable black Signifyin(g) difference” (xxiv). Subsumed within signifyin’ are several rhetorical tropes, including metaphor, synecdoche, metonymy, irony, and others, justifying Gates’s characterization of signifyin’ as the “black trope of all tropes, trope of tropes, the figure of figures.” Signifyin’, in its simplest definition, is “repetition and revision, or repetition with a signal difference” (xxiv). 10We might meditate here on the dominant culture’s propensity for digging into Black victims’ pasts and critiquing the victim’s appearance and/or degree of compliance to explain away police brutality, lynchings, and other racist policing practices. 11In an interview Boston community advocate Frederick Johnson gave to the Washington Post 30 years after the Stuart case, he recalled a particularly egregious night when he witnessed: “A group of at least 30 African American men, young to middle aged, were lying on the ground, stripped naked, their arms handcuffed behind their backs.” Johnson likened this scene to “how [slave traffickers] shackled slaves into ship galleys, one on top of the other,” adding “They were treated like animals” (Bernard).
204 R. SHARP


and psychological trauma that constitutes “another assault on the souls and honor of black folks” (Bernard). This ontological violence is also situated in abjection and Black non/Being; in the dehumaning in the preamble, present, and wake of racialized violence and oppression, in this spectacle of Black suffering. This abjection accommodates the complacency and complicity needed for White supremacy and racial capitalism to thrive comfortably on a steady fuel of Black pain. This violence is manifest in Mr. Zero’s name. Mr. Zero operates as a nameless, amorphic entity throughout much of the cycle; he is named only in the title of “The Unsigned Confession of Mr. Zero” (35). The name signifies Black non/Being in a direct way, Zero citing the value that Smith and her fellow racial hoaxers place on the Black lives of their plausible victims and their real and attempted social murders.12 Eady’s naming can be further read as an ironic riff on Odysseus’s “My name is Nobody” (Homer). Yet, whereas Odysseus’s trickery is the means by which some of his men and he escape the cyclops Polyphemus, the signifyin’ in Mr. Zero’s name is another means by which Eady exposes what had appeared to be a Black body was in actuality the monstrous shadow of – or, as Stamp Paid, put it, “the jungle” undergirding – the racial imaginary. Like Odysseus’s, Eady’s naming also orchestrates a sort of escape, yet through this naming, Eady is also enacting Black annotation and redaction as a means to blast what Sharpe calls the “dysgraphia and orthography of the wake” (115): his name correcting the racist narrative by underscoring his nonpresence as well as highlighting the layers of violence that result from its blatant disregard for Black lives. As such the “Brutal Imagination” cycle’s Black redaction subverts the racist annotation of Black bodies by figuratively snatching the Black out Smith’s mouth.13 Eady drives home this strategy in the direct Black annotation in “Birthing.” As the final poem of the cycle, “Birthing” joins “How I Got Born” not only as the cycle’s bookends, but “Birthing” also offers an interesting return to Mr. Zero’s origins (at least within the Smith episode): similar to the Gospels, the poems provide two takes on this particular nativity scene. Not to belabor this parallel, but it is interesting to consider how Smith reaches to find a more devious sense of salvation through her evocation of Mr. Zero’s image as well as her appeal for grace that very often is on the ready when a White woman’s tears are spilled. Through first merging Smith with Mr. Zero in “How I Got Born,” Eady’s pronoun use linguistically signals that the Black man Smith claimed to have stolen her car and her children is in actuality part of Smith’s racial imagination,
12Toward this end, GerShun Avilez connects Mr. Zero’s name to the concept of “zero image” that Black Arts era Carolyn Gerald theorizes in her 1976 essay, “The Black Writer and His Role.” Gerald’s “zero image” is very similar to Russell-Brown’s criminalblackman in how it denotes the racialized pathologized images of Blackness that White cultural producers project into the public sphere. Through this framing, Avilez notes that “The name Zero suggests the lack of identity and a reduced, abstracted existence” (55). 13Though I do not address this in the text of this article, it may be worth considering Eady’s riff on Greek mythology along with Morrison’s noted interest in, and frequent allusions to, the Classics.
WOMEN’S STUDIES 205


occupying her space though in an imagined Black body. Eady’s immediate observation that Smith is not the first to put Mr. Zero to work concurrently places Smith in a cultural and historical context and frames her as an individual representative of a larger whole – White supremacy – with her “brutal imagination” finding its genesis in the racial imaginary. This is further evidenced by Eady’s inclusion of other figures like Aunt Jemima, Uncles Ben and Tom, Buckwheat, and Stepin Fetchit in the cycle’s second section and his placement of the myth of the criminalblackman on the same plane as these other antiBlack caricatures. In doing so, Eady is able to draw into sharper focus the social conditions that invent and employ Mr. Zero as well as mammies, uncles, and dancing Sambos. This (dis)articulation sets up the spatial and grammatical separation in “Birthing.” The title – “Birthing” – establishes the poem’s irony, signaling early its signifyin’ potential. The term “birthing” brings to mind birthing people and motherhood and serves as a reminder of Smith’s status as a birthing person and, simultaneously, her betrayal of her motherhood, particularly with the poem reading her confession as being more concerned with her own mental and emotional state than the safety and well-being of her children. T he title also echoes the title of the first poem of the cycle, “How I Got Born,” and like “How I got Born” implies that the poem will provide Mr. Zero’s birth story, which they both do but with their own slant: whereas “How I Got Born” askews the notion of birth to describe how Smith conjures Mr. Zero, calls him to work, “Birthing” plays on yet another meaning for “birth”: extraction. In this way, the title foregrounds Mr. Zero’s removal of himself from the picture through Eady’s use of Black annotation and redaction in the poem, and in the “Brutal Imagination” cycle more broadly. In this way, the title, establishes how “Birthing” drives home the cycle’s (dis)articulation of Smith’s hoax, through pointing out her individual racism. Additionally, and more notably, the title also seizes on the kairotic moment Smith’s racist hoaxing creates in order to (counter-)employ her as a synecdoche for the more pernicious systemic racism that serves as the arbiter of the social death that emanates from the curation and exploitation of a Blackness the dominant culture finds palatable due its proximity to physical and/or social death. What is brought forward, then, in “Birthing” is not Mr. Zero’s Black body, but Smith’s, as well as the dominant culture’s, toxic Whiteness (itself bordering on, if not transgressing into, the pathological). In the body of the poem itself, Mr. Zero annotates excerpts from Smith’s hand-written confession. As such, he speaks himself into nonexistence. The call-and-response structure that annotates Smith’s confession with Mr. Zero’s signifyin’ back talk makes Eady’s/Mr. Zero’s annotation of Smith all the more visible, assigning their words different space and as well as a different typeface – with the excerpts from Smith’s confession appearing in italics. Whereas before the pronoun usage typically conflated Mr. Zero and Smith
206 R. SHARP


(typically within the same physical or imagined body), Eady disentangles the two when returning to the recollection of the night of Smith’s filicide paralleling the first three stanzas of “How I Got Born” – though this time through Smith’s perspective via her confession. Though it would seem that these visual cues would work to make Mr. Zero’s presence more readily apparent, Mr. Zero’s annotations foreground his absence. Before the poem’s final two lines, much of the descriptors used to describe Mr. Zero are there to remind the reader what he is not and where he is not. In the six couplets spoken through Mr. Zero in the first section of “Birthing,” there are five negations, four alone in the final two couplets, which read:
At this point, I have no language, No tongue, no mouth.
I am not me, yet. I am just an understanding. (53)
Of the nine sections that follow, five include at least one negation that result a similar trend that highlights Mr. Zero’s physical nonexistence. The negations in Mr. Zero again bring to mind lynching erasure artwork and how the redaction of the Black body draws Whiteness into sharp(er) focus. This structure – using annotation to redact – enhances the poem’s irony and, thus, its ability draw our eye to Smith and how she metonymically represents the systemic forces that sustain racial hoaxes. It is at this point, the focus on this particular disappearance, that the poem’s irony is best revealed. The mind might be tempted to jump to Smith’s inconsistencies, narcissism, and White tears. It is tempting to focus on how Smith seems to describe how things happened suddenly and juxtapose that with how a reenactment showed that it likely took six minutes for her car to submerge. It is tempting to get in the weeds around how some studies show that high stress situations impact one’s perception of time. Yet, that is not what Eady is doing here. Instead, he is redacting the presence of a Black body, while highlighting Smith’s White body. He is lingering in the space in between her confession statements, not as places of Black aspiration – in fact Mr. Zero has yet to breathe – but as a site for executing the extraction of the Black body from Smith’s letter – highlighting the absence of Blackness – while alluding to its fixation in her imagination; it being so fixed, or conditioned, there that even under extreme duress and stress and all that Smith describes, she was able to understand and exploit the racist logic that people would most readily accept her story if she hangs it on a Black body. This trajectory – from merging Mr. Zero with Smith to then pointing out Mr. Zero’s lack of physical existence – is made especially effective, if not possible, through the irony created by Eady’s use of nonhuman personae as
WOMEN’S STUDIES 207


a commentary on Black non/being. In doing so, Eady is able to play on Mr. Zero’s literal lack of being, while critically reading the ontological violence inherent in Smith’s racial hoax that was able to persist not because of the airtightness of Smith’s lie, but due to the dominant culture’s familiar relationship with and committed investment in the myth of the criminalblackman. As a result of its redaction of the Black body from this equation and annotation of its utter Whiteness, this reading renders toxic Whiteness and its creation of and fixation on Blackness made palatable by its relation to physical and/or social death visible and legible. Morrison has made clear that her aim is to center Black peoples, and, in doing so, she often offered an oppositional gaze, even while refusing to privilege Whiteness. While there are plenty of places where we might see Morrison’s influence, her wake, broadly within U.S. American literature as well as more locally within the 21stcentury brand of Black U.S. American persona poetry and its engagement with dominant narratives, this is where Morrison shows through the most in this particular work: in Eady’s effective utilization of her model for employing the nonhuman to do decolonizing (wake) work. It is through employing Morrison’s tactics that the “Brutal Imagination” cycle is able to write off the racist stamp from the Black body and reassign it to the racist itself, placing the pathology where it actually exists, in the dominant culture’s perpetuation of, dependence on, and investment in racist U.S. institutions and structures and not in particular minoritized communities.
